---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/russell-decisive/30min';

const title = 'Finance for the trades | Decisive';
const description =
  'A sale price is a multiple times earnings. We grow the margin your crews earn and make it provable in the numbers a buyer tests, in 14 days.';

const traps = [
  {
    name: 'Anchoring',
    body: 'Bids priced to costs that moved two years ago read as margin you gave away. Anchored pricing is the most common trapped margin a buyer re-prices against you, job by job.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Status Quo',
    body: 'Sub invoices and material receipts that never land on the job that spent them mean the true cost of a job shows up 60 days after closeout. A buyer sees WIP nobody can defend, and discounts the whole backlog.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Loss Aversion',
    body: 'The general contractor account you are most afraid to reprice is often the one capping your margin, and the concentration a buyer discounts hardest. Naming it early is worth real multiple.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">For owner-led trades: construction, HVAC, electrical, plumbing</span>
      <h1>
        <span class="h1-line">You can't sell a trades business on numbers</span>
        <span class="h1-line h1-line-accent">a buyer can't tie to the jobs.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="hero-subhead">
        A sale price is a multiple times earnings. <span class="dollar">We work both numbers:</span> we grow the margin your crews earn, and we make it provable in the WIP, retainage, and job costs a buyer will test.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>Book a fit call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-it-works">See the 14-day Scan</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-microcopy">30 minutes with Russ. No pitch. You leave knowing what a buyer would re-price, and what you could recover.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">What a buyer re-prices in the trades</span>
        <h2>Your crews earn the margin. Your numbers have to prove it.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A buyer of a trades business is buying a backlog and a track record of hitting it. They test WIP accuracy, over and under-billing, retainage, the quality of the backlog, how much of the company is the owner, and whether job costs are real. Every soft answer is a discount, and every one is also margin you can recover before you go to market.
        </p>
        <div class="category-pull">
          Accurate and ugly beats polished and untrustworthy. Buyers know the difference.
        </div>
        <p>
          The mess isn't negligence. Growth covered it, and the people producing the numbers were never paid to look. Everyone who touches your books closes the month. Nobody is making your company worth believing, and that is the job we do. The day a sale, a PE approach, or a succession comes into view, every soft number converts from a chronic annoyance into priced risk, and the clock starts.
        </p>
        <div class="delay-callout">
          The trailing-twelve clock doesn't start until the numbers are true. Every month you wait moves the sale date a month.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="traps">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Where the value is trapped</div>
      <h2>Three traps a buyer looks for first.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.88);">
        The Six Trap Diagnostic&trade; reads your numbers the way a buyer will. Three of the six do the most damage in the trades. The patterns are structural, not failures of the owner.
      </p>
      <div class="trap-grid">
        {traps.map((t, i) => (
          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-num">Trap {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            <div class="trap-name">{t.name}</div>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="traps-footer">
        We name the stack, price each one two ways, what a buyer discounts and what you can recover, and turn the read into a plan. We don't audit the past. We make the company worth believing.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Proof</span>
        <h2>The mechanism reads the same across industries, because the traps do.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="worked">
        <span class="label">Manufacturer, $15M revenue</span>
        <h4>Concentration a buyer discounts is margin you can recover before you go to market.</h4>
        <p>
          The biggest account felt untouchable, so the math never got run, and the concentration was exactly what a buyer discounts hardest. Full cost allocation showed it was funded by everyone else. We repriced and reset terms instead of protecting the logo. The same pattern lives in a contractor's largest GC relationship.
        </p>
        <div class="calc">
          <div><span class="k">Least profitable account</span><span class="v accent">The largest one</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Margins moved</span><span class="v">15% to 22%</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Time to recover</span><span class="v">90 days</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 22px;">
        Anonymized by segment and revenue band until attribution is confirmed. Trades outcomes available in conversation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Tiers and pricing</span>
        <h2>Find. Install. Run.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most owners start with the 14-day Sellable-Numbers Scan and move into the 90-Day Resolution. Pricing is banded by revenue, and the floor is $5M.
        </p>
        <p class="promise-line">
          The Scan promise: at least 3x the fee in owner-accepted value identified by Day 14, or you pay nothing.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta-row" style="margin-top: 8px;">
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/#tiers">See tiers and pricing</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guarantee">Read the guarantee</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="close-section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">Book a fit call</span>
      <h2>Decide from the numbers. Prove it in the cash.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        In 14 days you see your company the way a buyer will, in dollars, on both sides of the equation. You leave with the re-price read, the worth read, and the first three moves, whether or not we ever work together again. It starts with one 30-minute call.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>Book a fit call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-it-works">See the 14-day Scan</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero-microcopy { font-size: 15px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7); margin: 10px 0 0; }
  .promise-line { font-family: Inter, sans-serif; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
